<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Supplemental Info.plist keys.

  This file carries only what cannot be expressed as an INFOPLIST_KEY_ build
  setting, which is anything with an array or dictionary value. Everything else
  is generated (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES) and merged with these.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The Mizar transport is a cleartext WebSocket to an address the operator
	  supplies at pairing time: a LAN address or a relay URL. ATS decides
	  at build time which hosts it trusts, and the host here is not knowable in
	  advance, so there is no exception that could name it.

	  The honest fix is TLS on the listener, which is already a planned item; it
	  removes the need for this key entirely. Until then, the traffic's
	  confidentiality comes from the network underneath — WireGuard in the
	  relay case, the local segment in the LAN case.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!-- Required for iOS 14 and later when connecting to a local-network host. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>DSH-Mizar connects to your computer on the local network to monitor and guide your agent.</string>
	<!--
	  Declared because the app browses for it. iOS raises the local-network
	  permission prompt when an app first *uses* the local network, and a Bonjour
	  browse for an undeclared service type is answered with silence rather than
	  with the prompt -- so the type has to be here for the ask to happen at all.

	  Nothing has to advertise it. The browse exists to raise the question before
	  the operator is halfway through scanning a pairing code, not to find
	  anything: a computer on the same Wi-Fi is reached at the address in the QR.
	-->
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_mizar._tcp</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The pairing URI is mizar://pair?...  Registering the scheme makes the QR
	  payload work as a link as well as as a barcode: an operator who receives it
	  in a message can tap it and be paired, without opening the camera.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>me.shirazu.dshmizar.pair</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>mizar</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The app uses only HTTPS and standard TLS through URLSession, which is exempt
	  from export documentation. Declaring it here answers the export compliance
	  question at upload instead of leaving a build stuck in "Missing Compliance".
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- The pairing QR is scanned in-app, so the camera is used only for that. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Scan the pairing code shown on your computer.</string>
	<!--
	  Live Activities, declared on the app rather than on the extension.

	  This is the key that decides whether this app may post one at all: without
	  it `Activity.request` fails with `unsupported`, and a widget extension that
	  is present and correct changes nothing. It belongs to the *app* because the
	  app is what asks for the activity; the extension only draws the one it is
	  handed.

	  `NSSupportsLiveActivitiesFrequentUpdates` is deliberately **not** here. That
	  key is for activities whose content is pushed at a high rate -- alerts, a
	  score changing every few seconds -- and this activity's one moving number is
	  a `Text(_:style:.timer)` that the *system* ticks, with no update from the app
	  at all. What the app sends is a change of state: a session appeared, a
	  session stopped, or a fresh figure arrived from the computer. Claiming frequent
	  updates would be asking for a budget nothing here spends.
	-->
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The periodic refresh, declared where the system looks for it.

	  `BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers` is the allow-list: `register` for an
	  identifier that is not here returns false, and the app would then look
	  perfectly correct while never being woken. The string is the same one
	  `BackgroundRefresh.refreshIdentifier` holds -- the two are checked against
	  each other by the log line at launch, which is the only way a mismatch is
	  visible at all.

	  What this buys, honestly: while the app is off screen, iOS may wake it to
	  re-read the session list and update an activity that is already up. It is
	  not a schedule and it is not a promise. The system decides when, from a
	  hint (`earliestBeginDate`) and its own budget for this app, and it can
	  decide never. No refresh can *start* an activity on a phone whose app has
	  not been opened -- that needs push-to-start, which this build does not
	  have. See `BackgroundRefresh` in the app target.
	-->
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>me.shirazu.dshmizar.refresh</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  "Background fetch" is the mode `BGAppRefreshTask` belongs to. Without it
	  the request is submitted and never runs, which is the quietest failure mode
	  in this whole file.

	  Nothing else is declared: the app does not play audio, use location, or
	  keep a VoIP socket, and a background mode is a claim to the App Store that
	  the app needs one of those.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>fetch</string>
		<!-- A silent turn-end push is the last chance to take down a card whose
		     activity token never reached the host. Without this mode iOS can
		     deliver the notification but cannot wake the app to end it. -->
		<string>remote-notification</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The local agent keeps its workspaces in Documents, and the operator is
	  meant to be able to look at them: `UIFileSharingEnabled` puts the folder in
	  the Files app ("On My iPhone > DSH-Mizar"), and
	  `LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace` is what makes the app open those files
	  in place instead of copying them through a temporary directory. Neither key
	  is used by the remote half of the app, whose files stay in Application
	  Support.
	-->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Animations may run faster than 60 Hz on an iPhone with a ProMotion display.
	  Without this key the system may hold an app's animations to 60 Hz, whatever
	  frame rate they ask for -- and the drawers ask for 120. A boolean, but here
	  rather than a build setting: Xcode has no INFOPLIST_KEY_ for it.
	-->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The two schemes the 社区 section asks about before it opens anything.

	  `canOpenURL` answers "no" for every scheme that is not declared here, so
	  without this list the QQ row could never tell an installed QQ from an
	  absent one and would always fall back to copying the group number. Both
	  spellings are declared because QQ registers both -- `mqqapi` for the card
	  links this app uses, `mqq` for the older ones a different QQ build may
	  answer.
	-->
	<key>LSApplicationQueriesSchemes</key>
	<array>
		<string>mqqapi</string>
		<string>mqq</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
